[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186115206 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115036 :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x)=\\ln (2x+4)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y = \\ln x$ is\n\n$$\nx \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x+4 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-4 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = f(x)$ is $x\\in\\left( -2, \\infty \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115038 :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = -\\ln(4x).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y = \\ln{x}$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115207 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e7e97e3-f06a-5ac7-917d-a28a5b06bd45"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115037 :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\log(2x) - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\log(2x) - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =2 \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =100 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=50.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" "question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115031 :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = 5\\log_6(x-1)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y = \\log_{6}(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nFor a logarithmic function, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb" :question/id #uuid "1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-41247" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{7}⁡(2x-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y={log}_{7}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x-3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f697687-ec84-5c60-ac6b-c8ef620378f1" "answer-eafff2db-2dce-5e16-ad27-40dcf2655fe9" "answer-d4a03f4b-ffe0-5cc9-94bd-21afd40d50db" "answer-1c8bb634-5f7a-526b-b108-ac68b8ec6d79" "answer-9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f697687-ec84-5c60-ac6b-c8ef620378f1" :answer/id #uuid "5f697687-ec84-5c60-ac6b-c8ef620378f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eafff2db-2dce-5e16-ad27-40dcf2655fe9" :answer/id #uuid "eafff2db-2dce-5e16-ad27-40dcf2655fe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4a03f4b-ffe0-5cc9-94bd-21afd40d50db" :answer/id #uuid "d4a03f4b-ffe0-5cc9-94bd-21afd40d50db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c8bb634-5f7a-526b-b108-ac68b8ec6d79" :answer/id #uuid "1c8bb634-5f7a-526b-b108-ac68b8ec6d79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef" :answer/id #uuid "9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234035 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)-4.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $\\frac{x}{2}-1$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & >1 \\\\ x & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29b036b2-53a7-5982-a624-2d6079e0bb8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29b036b2-53a7-5982-a624-2d6079e0bb8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "29b036b2-53a7-5982-a624-2d6079e0bb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3550efc7-35cb-5814-94b8-fede4a088302" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6beda0ec-d95b-5c6d-8778-b960e0ffbf50" "answer-3550efc7-35cb-5814-94b8-fede4a088302" "answer-a36986d0-a604-5b40-87fb-231cd29bf444" "