$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}⁡(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4"] ["answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/id #uuid "f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1"] ["answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/value "x=1"] ["answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/id #uuid "ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42"] ["answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/value "x=16"] ["answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/id #uuid "d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b"] ["answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/value "x=4"] ["answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/id #uuid "ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d"] ["answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/value "x=6"] ["answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/id #uuid "e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e"] ["answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/value "x=8"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/correct "answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/choices "answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/choices "answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/choices "answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/choices "answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d"] ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/choices "answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e"] [17592186234440 :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/id #uuid "800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/math-academy-id "q-81862"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-3.$"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{2}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/id #uuid "607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33"] ["answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" :answer/id #uuid "b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24"] ["answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-3,\\infty )"] ["answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" :answer/id #uuid "965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8"] ["answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-4,\\infty )"] ["answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" :answer/id #uuid "1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599"] ["answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4)"] ["answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer/id #uuid "20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3"] ["answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce" :answer/id #uuid "2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce"] ["answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce" :answer/value "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/correct "answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/choices "answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/choices "answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/choices "answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/choices "answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3"] ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/choices "answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce"] ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33"] [17592186115202 :knowledge-point/questions "question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521"] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/id #uuid "cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94"] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/math-academy-id "q-166551"] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)+3.$"] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/id #uuid "55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0"] ["answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" :answer/id #uuid "91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e"] ["answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" :answer/value "1"] ["answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" :answer/id #uuid "294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81"] ["answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" :answer/value "3"] ["answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" :answer/id #uuid "cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4"] ["answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" :answer/id #uuid "6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7"] ["answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer/id #uuid "6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785"] ["answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/correct "answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/choices "answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/choices "answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/choices "answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/choices "answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7"] ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/choices "answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785"] ["question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0"] [17592186115202 :knowledge-point/questions "question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94"] [17592186116480 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = \\log_2(x-1).$ What is the domain of $f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of $y=\\log_a (x)$ is\n\n$$\nx > 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x).$ In this case, the argument is $x-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty).$"] [17592186116527 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1.$ \n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n\n$$\n\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$"] [17592186115436 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186115036 :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x)=\\ln (2x+4)-2$?"] [17592186115036 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y = \\ln x$ is\n$x \\gt 0$.\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)$, we replace $x$ with $2x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n$2x+4 > 0$.\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 4 & > 0 \\\\ 2x & > - 4 \\\\ x & > - 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left(-2, \\infty \\right)\n$$"] [17592186115038 :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = -\\ln(4x)$."] [17592186115038 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y = \\ln{x}$ is $x=0$.\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = 0$."] [17592186115037 :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\log(2x) - 2$."] [17592186115037 :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\log(2x) - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (2x) - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\log (2x) &= 2 \\\\\n10^{\\log (2x)} &= 10^{2} \\\\\n2x &= 100 \\\\\nx &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=50$."] [17592186115031 :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = 5\\log_6(x-1)-4$?"] [17592186115031 :question/worked-solution "The range of the function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_{6}(x)\n$$\n\nis $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nFor a logarithmic function, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$."] [17592186234035 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{x}{2}-1)-4.$"] [17592186234035 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{log}x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $\\frac{x}{2}-1$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & >1 \\\\ x & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$"] [17592186234436 :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(2x)-8.$"] [17592186234436 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"] [17592186234437 :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=10\\operatorname{ln}(5x).$"] [17592186234437 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"] [17592186234440 :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}(x)-6.$"] [17592186234440 :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$"] [17592186116480 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\log_2(x-1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of $f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nis\n$x > 0$.\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)$, we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x)$. In this case, the argument is $x-1$, so we get\n$x-1 > 0$.\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x - 1 & > 0 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty)$."] [17592186116527 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n$\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$"]]}]